# rldora.msg - Dora Quist, madam of the Trough (NCR, Licensed Establishment No. 9)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

# Report and payout
{100}{}{Number Nine, examinations up to date, papers in order. The caravans came through on schedule. Here's the week.}
{121}{}{. The Republic takes its fifteen percent before we even see it. That's what's left.}
{122}{}{Nothing. Tax day and fee day came in the same week.}

# Prices and share
{130}{}{Drovers pay in cash and never twice the same week. Cheap brings in the ones who spend their pay before they reach the Hub. Dear brings the brahmin barons.}
{140}{}{Our people keep }
{141}{}{ percent of what they bring in. The Republic says that's a fair wage for a licensed trade. The Republic has never done the trade.}
{145}{}{A quarter. (Dora snorts.) That's what Vortis leaves the people in his pens. Just so you know.}
{146}{}{A third. Like a caravan guard. Nobody gets rich, nobody runs off into the desert.}
{147}{}{Almost half? (She laughs out loud.) Then I'll have to beat the drovers off the doorstep with a ladle.}

# Morale
{150}{}{They eat together in the kitchen and argue about caravans. Like family, only nobody owes anybody.}
{151}{}{They do the work and the paperwork. In the Republic the paperwork is worse.}
{152}{}{They look at Vortis' pens every morning when they come in. They know how close it is.}
{153}{}{One of them is gone. Took a caravan north. I packed her some food.}

# Crisis: the drug
{160}{}{One of ours is on Jet. The drovers bring it from Redding. She's skipping examinations so nobody sees her arms.}
{165}{}{(Dora watches you work.) The Republic's doctors wouldn't have come to Number Nine for that. You did.}
{166}{}{There's a clinic in Downtown that takes Jet cases. They ask questions, but they help.}
{167}{}{(Dora slams the ladle down.) Jet instead of wages. That's Vortis' way. I'll do it, and I'll write down every week I do it.}
{168}{}{Out, then. She'll be in Vortis' pens by winter. That's where everybody ends up who falls through here.}

# Look
{200}{}{A broad woman with a cook's forearms and a pencil behind her ear.}
{201}{}{Dora Quist. Twenty years cooking for caravans. Now she runs Licensed Establishment Number Nine.}

# Construction and staff (her voice)
{460}{}{What do we build? The Republic wants order. So first an infirmary, then papers, then everything else.}
{463}{}{Something you only get in the Republic.}
{483}{}{Nobody's looking for new faces.}
{484}{}{Our recruiter works the caravan yards. Women who came with a wagon and have no reason to leave with it.}
{485}{}{Our recruiter buys debts in the Bazaar. Some of those debts are owed to Vortis. You know what that means.}
{490}{}{I know every cook and drover's widow in the Bazaar. The ones who want a roof and a register will come.}
{491}{}{(Dora doesn't look up from her list.) Debts from the Bazaar. So we're buying from the same market as Vortis now. I'll keep the receipts. The Rangers read receipts.}

# First meeting
{800}{}{(A broad woman is writing names on a slate by the door.) Dora Quist. I cooked for caravans for twenty years; I know every drover between Redding and the Hub, and which ones pay. House rules: everyone is registered, everyone gets examined, everyone gets paid in cash on Friday. The Republic likes rules. So do I, if they're mine.}
{801}{}{Show me how the house is doing.}

# The League (Die Reinen)
{850}{}{The League for a Pure Republic.}
{851}{}{The League's pickets are at the door every morning. Half the drovers turn around when they see the signs. Calloway won't be bought, but her leaflets can be drowned out. Or her pickets can be run off.}
{870}{}{The campaign is where it is. Now we wait for the Council.}
{871}{}{[Barter] Get the Rawhide and the caravan bosses to pay for leaflets of our own.}
{872}{}{[Unarmed] Run the pickets off.}
{873}{}{The Rawhide paid, the caravan masters paid, and by Friday there are more of our leaflets in the Bazaar than hers. Half the drovers come back.}
{874}{}{(The pickets scatter. One of them doesn't get up for a long time.) The campaign's over. The League has a martyr now, and the Council will hear about it.}
{855}{}{The Council will vote on a decency law that would revoke every license in the Republic. You can speak before the Council, buy the votes, or get Westin to speak for us. Or let them decide without us.}
{875}{}{The Council has heard what it's going to hear. Now we wait.}
{876}{}{[Speech] I'll speak before the Council myself.}
{877}{}{[Barter] Buy enough votes. ($2000)}
{878}{}{[Speech] Get Westin to speak for us.}
{879}{}{Remind Westin what he says in our private rooms.}
{881}{}{Let the Council decide without us.}
{882}{}{(You speak for an hour. About the pens next door, about examinations, about the difference between a license and a chain. When you finish, nobody claps, but three councilors change their notes.)}
{883}{}{(Two thousand dollars in eleven envelopes. The vote will go our way.)}
{884}{}{(But one of the envelopes ends up on Calloway's desk. By evening the whole Republic knows the price of a councilor.)}
{885}{}{(Westin stands up in the Council and talks about ranch hands who need somewhere to go on a Saturday. He doesn't look at you once.)}
{886}{}{(Westin reads the note twice, then burns it. The next day he speaks for the Trough in the Council, and he hates you for every word.)}
{887}{}{(The Council hears the law without anyone speaking for Number Nine.)}
{862}{}{The back is burning! Somebody threw a firebomb through the window. If we don't stop it, the whole upper floor goes.}
{888}{}{[Repair] Get the pump working and get water up there.}
{889}{}{[Traps] There's another firebomb somewhere. I'll find it first.}
{891}{}{Everybody says it was the League. I don't believe what everybody says.}
{892}{}{[Science] Let me look at what they used to start it.}
{893}{}{I'll ask around the Bazaar. Somebody saw something.}
{894}{}{[Sneak, Lockpick] Vortis' office. If there's anything written down, it's there.}
{895}{}{(Water, sand and wet blankets. The back room is black, but the house stands.)}
{896}{}{(It's industrial fuel, the kind only one Bazaar trader sells, and only to Vortis. A beggar saw men from the pens at the back window. In Vortis' desk there's a note: "Number Nine. Tonight. Make it look like the League.") It was Vortis. The League fights the slave trade too. A fire they get blamed for would get them banned.}
{897}{}{(The fuel, the witness, the note: all of it leads to a handful of League members who stopped believing in leaflets. Calloway didn't know. She wouldn't have allowed it. But it was her people.) After what you've done with Vortis, they decided you were the pens with better furniture. There's no clean side to this.}
{898}{}{What do we do with it?}
{899}{}{We have proof: Vortis set the fire. We can give it to the Rangers, keep it and make Vortis pay, or change a few names and hang it on the League.}
{900}{}{We have proof: a radical wing of the League set the fire. We can give it to the Rangers, or make it Calloway's fire, not just her people's.}
{901}{}{Give it to the Rangers.}
{902}{}{Keep it. Vortis will pay to keep it quiet. (150 a week)}
{903}{}{[Science] Change the names. Make it the League's fire.}
{904}{}{(The Rangers take the proof. Vortis is arrested, his pens are opened, and the League owes you publicly. When the decency law passes, it comes with an exception with our number on it: Lex Nine. Calloway will check our books every month. Fair.)}
{905}{}{(It works. The League is finished, and Ruth Calloway is ruined. Nobody in the Bazaar hands out leaflets anymore. Nobody asks what happened to the people who did.)}
{906}{}{(The decency law fails in the Council. The League is still out there, but quieter. We keep our license. Nobody wins.)}
{907}{}{(The decency law passes without exceptions. Number Nine has no license anymore. We keep working. Everything illegal in the Republic keeps working.)}
{880}{}{Vortis sent a man. He has people in his pens and room in his prices. ($200 a head)}
{908}{}{(Dora writes a name on the slate and underlines it twice.) She doesn't speak Republic. She doesn't speak at all. If the Rangers ever see her papers, we're finished.}
{890}{}{Make sure Ruth Calloway never speaks again.}
{909}{}{(Two days later Ruth Calloway is found behind the Council Hall. The Rangers ask questions all over the Bazaar. Dora doesn't talk to you for a week.)}

